Gateway configuration file downloads
====================================

The gateway System Download Config action now saves the content returned by
the configuration API. It no longer interprets that response as a device-write
result. Text is saved as XML, preserving its UTF-8 bytes and BOM; an explicitly
base64-encoded OpenWRT archive is decoded and saved as tar.gz. Gateway response
identity, recognized format, nonempty content, canonical base64 and a 16 MiB
original-content bound are checked before a browser object URL is created.
The filename contains only a fixed prefix, the validated gateway UUID and the
recognized extension. Adapter-supplied filenames and vendor strings are not
used in the filename. Temporary anchors and object URLs are released.

The backup table exposes one Export configuration action, including when its
history is empty. The old icon on every history row was misleading: every row
called the same generic endpoint without passing the selected backup identity.
Those icons are removed. Export does not claim to download a historical row.
OPNsense still supplies its latest device backup; see OPNSENSE-CONFIG-DOWNLOAD.

Only one export request is active per gateway page. Both buttons are disabled
while it is pending; repeated callbacks are also guarded. Changing gateway or
unmounting aborts the request and ignores late completion. Responses are not
placed in React Query or mutation caches. UI errors are fixed descriptions,
without backend diagnostic text. A successful browser initiation says Download
started and warns that the file may contain credentials. It does not promise
that the user completed an operating-system Save dialog or stored it securely.
New messages use explicit English fallbacks pending additional translations.

This change does not create a durable backup service, historical content API,
retention policy, restoration proof, or caller/resource authority checks after
device I/O. Archive representation is not proof of tar integrity or restore
compatibility. The separate legacy backup trigger still needs durable capture.

Qualification includes 13 actual-page checks with nine reproduced old failures
and four controls, 32 document/URL checks, and two missing-ID lifecycle checks.
All 842 frontend tests, lint and strict production build pass. Nine actual
Chromium cases exercise the real page and Axios over loopback HTTP, compare
downloaded bytes, and check failed/stale/duplicate requests. API responses and
identity are synthetic; no device authentication or hardware backup restoration
is claimed. The first nullable cleanup build failure and malformed fixture
pending-changes response are retained with their corrections.
